<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Malacca Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1511 CE (approx.) · capital Malacca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great Malay trading sultanate on the Strait of Malacca, whose 15th-century port linked China, India and the spice islands until Portugal took it in 1511.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, a prince who had fled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-singapura">Kingdom of Singapura</a>, founded a settlement at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> River. He won the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a>, receiving the visits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He's</a> fleets and travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> himself in 1411, which shielded the port from Siam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca's</a> rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> early in the 15th century, and the port became a centre for the faith's spread. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-of-malacca">Muzaffar Shah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-shah-of-malacca">Mansur Shah</a>, and the long-serving bendahara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, it ruled both sides of the Strait. Harbour masters handled merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and its legal codes and court etiquette became models for later Malay states.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured the city, founding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mahmud Shah</a> fled, and his sons founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak Sultanate</a>, continuing the dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> record <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca's</a> glory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/malacca-sultanate.jpg" alt="Malacca Sultanate" width="320" height="364"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malacca_Sultanate_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1511 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malacca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Parameswara, Mansur Shah, Mahmud Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Melaka Sultanate, Sultanate of Malacca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1456&amp;focus=malacca-sultanate" aria-label="Malacca Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7519 2096 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Malacca Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7839 2352L7833 2434M7839 2352L7883 2385M7839 2352L7643 1242M7839 2352L7752 1900M7839 2352L7843 2352M7839 2352L7881 2378M7839 2352L7870 2303M7839 2352L7799 2255"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M7883 2385h0M7643 1242h0M7752 1900h0M7843 2352h0M7881 2378h0M7870 2303h0M7799 2255h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7839 2352h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7839 2352h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Malacca. <a href="/atlas?year=1456&amp;focus=malacca-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
